    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots cleanly into the established pattern. I compared it against the sibling `RejectDuplicateTelephone` feature and it mirrors it exactly, which is the right call for a maintainer of this codebase:\n\n- **Right unit of logic.** The duplicate-email check lives in its own `RejectDuplicateEmail` function class with a single `service(@Val Owner, OwnerRepository)` method \u2014 matching the one-public-method-per-class convention and the existing `Reject<...>` family (`RejectDuplicateTelephone`, `RejectDuplicateHousehold`, `RejectCityAtCapacity`, \u2026). It isn't bolted onto `NormalizeOwnerEmail` or `SaveOwner`, which would have made those into catch-alls.\n- **No duplication.** The exception (`DuplicateEmailException extends Exception`), the handler (`ProblemDetails.build(..., CONFLICT, ...)` \u2192 409), and the escalation YAML are structurally identical to the telephone equivalents \u2014 the intended, consistent shape, not copy-paste that should have been generalized. These are genuinely distinct error cases, so separate exception types are correct.\n- **Idiomatic wiring.** Checked exception so it surfaces in `throws`; escalation YAML named by fully-qualified exception; `rejectDuplicateEmail` step wired with `govern: [ transaction ]` and `next:`, inserted after normalization and before the other reject steps \u2014 consistent with every neighbouring step.\n- **Naming and placement.** `Reject<Entity><Condition>` verb-plus-entity naming, correct `function.owner` / `escalation` packages, DTOs kept out (works on the `Owner` entity). The Javadoc follows the same \"Runs after \u2026 before SaveOwner\" format as its sibling, and correctly notes email is optional (the null/blank skip matches `NormalizeOwnerEmail`'s own optionality handling).\n\nNothing is in the wrong place, nothing is becoming a catch-all, and the structure reads as a natural extension of the existing create pipeline.",
